Q: U-boot recipe failing to apply patch A: <p>Your patches are almost certainly applying fine. A patch that fails in <code>do_patch</code> aborts the build, so if you ended up with an image at all, it applied. <code>bitbake -e | grep SRC_URI</code> only proves the patches are listed, not applied — if you want to see the real source, <code>bitbake u-boot-toradex -c devshell</code> and look at the board file. One devtool gotcha to rule out: if the recipe is still in your workspace (<code>devtool status</code> shows it), the build uses the workspace tree via externalsrc and the patches in your bbappend are skipped entirely.</p>
<p>The actual bug is in your gpio numbers, and your own console output shows it:</p>
<pre><code>gpio: pin GPIO0_25 (gpio 33) value is 0
</code></pre>
<p>GPIO0_25 is global number <strong>33</strong>, not 25. There is no <code>32*chip + line</code> rule in U-Boot — the flat numbers get assigned per bank in driver-model registration order, and on your board the first bank doesn't even start at 0. So <code>gpio_request(25, &quot;SODIMM_PIN_103&quot;)</code> is driving some other ball entirely, and the same goes for the rest of your table. Which fits what you're seeing: the patch probably <em>is</em> running, just toggling the wrong pins.</p>
<p>Either look pins up by name in board code:</p>
<pre><code>struct gpio_desc desc;

if (!dm_gpio_lookup_name(&quot;GPIO0_25&quot;, &amp;desc)) {
    dm_gpio_request(&amp;desc, &quot;SODIMM_103&quot;);
    dm_gpio_set_dir_flags(&amp;desc, GPIOD_IS_OUT);   /* driven low */
}
</code></pre>
<p>or skip the board code patch entirely and use gpio-hogs in the U-Boot device tree (<code>CONFIG_GPIO_HOG=y</code>):</p>
<pre><code>&amp;lsio_gpio0 {
    sodimm-103-hog {
        gpio-hog;
        gpios = &lt;25 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH&gt;;
        output-low;
    };
};
</code></pre>
<p>Hogs are applied when the bank binds during the DM scan, which is even a bit earlier than <code>board_init()</code>, and they address pins as bank + offset so the numbering problem goes away.</p>
<p>About the missing prints: they don't prove anything. In 2024.04 <code>board_init()</code> runs before <code>serial_initialize</code>/<code>console_init_r</code> (see <code>init_sequence_r[]</code> in <code>common/board_r.c</code>), and <code>board_init_r()</code> clears <code>GD_FLG_SERIAL_READY</code> on entry, so output from there gets dropped unless you have <code>CONFIG_DEBUG_UART</code>. <code>log_debug()</code> is compiled out without <code>CONFIG_LOG</code> anyway. Put your marker print in <code>board_late_init()</code> if you want to actually see it. And do check <code>ver</code> at the prompt — on these modules the bootloader is the imx-boot container in the eMMC boot area, and re-deploying your image without re-flashing it (Easy Installer) happily keeps booting the old U-Boot.</p>
<p>Last thing, since you want these pins low at startup: everything U-Boot does happens ~1s after power-on, and on i.MX8QXP the SCU/SCFW owns the pads before U-Boot even starts. If these pins must never glitch high, fix it with pull-downs in the pad config or external resistors — software can only shorten the high window, not remove it.</p>

Q: How to call a C++ lambda from a constexpr function? A: <p>It's not the lambda that has non-trivial destructor, it's <code>std::function</code>.</p>
<p><a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/utility/functional/function" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::function</code></a> is a rather heavy-weight class (compared to other callables) that implements <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Type_erasure" rel="nofollow noreferrer">type erasure</a> to allow storing any callable you want (including function pointers, lambdas with or without capture, functor objects and other). That includes allocating memory, which excludes it from being declared as <code>constexpr</code> object (unless you have C++20 and the entire object lifetime is within one statement).</p>
<p>Make the type of <code>my_lambda</code> be deduced to actual underlying lambda type and it works with either <code>const</code> or <code>constexpr</code>.</p>
<pre><code>const auto my_lambda = [] (const int x) -&gt; int
{
    return x;
};

constexpr int foo(int x)
{
    return my_lambda(x);
}

int main()
{
    int x = 10;
    return foo(x);
}
</code></pre>
